1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,471
Look at almost any big cargo ship

2
00:00:02,471 --> 00:00:04,644
in port, and you will see the

3
00:00:04,644 --> 00:00:05,468
same thing.

4
00:00:05,648 --> 00:00:07,501
Black or blue on top,

5
00:00:07,501 --> 00:00:09,620
and a band of red below,

6
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:11,650
right down to the keel.

7
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:14,108
Container ships, tankers,

8
00:00:14,108 --> 00:00:17,206
bulk carriers, all red underneath.

9
00:00:17,386 --> 00:00:18,825
It is not a fashion.

10
00:00:19,005 --> 00:00:20,837
That red is a weapon,

11
00:00:20,837 --> 00:00:23,802
in a war against tiny animals that

12
00:00:23,802 --> 00:00:25,983
has lasted for centuries.

13
00:00:26,163 --> 00:00:29,370
Today, ten parts of a cargo ship's

14
00:00:29,370 --> 00:00:32,200
hull, and why each one exists.

15
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:34,563
The ocean is full of life,

16
00:00:34,563 --> 00:00:36,732
and much of it wants to live

17
00:00:36,732 --> 00:00:37,507
on a ship.

18
00:00:37,687 --> 00:00:41,392
Barnacles, mussels, weed and slime all grow

19
00:00:41,392 --> 00:00:44,235
on any surface left in the water.

20
00:00:44,415 --> 00:00:45,174
On a ship,

21
00:00:45,174 --> 00:00:47,453
that growth is called fouling,

22
00:00:47,453 --> 00:00:49,352
and it is one of the most

23
00:00:49,352 --> 00:00:51,706
expensive problems in shipping.

24
00:00:52,056 --> 00:00:55,083
As always, every fact here comes from

25
00:00:55,083 --> 00:00:56,310
a named source,

26
00:00:56,310 --> 00:00:59,910
maritime organisations and naval references,

27
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:02,365
all listed in the description.

28
00:01:02,545 --> 00:01:03,904
Let's start with the red.

29
00:01:04,254 --> 00:01:06,820
Why are cargo ships red below the

30
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:07,597
waterline?

31
00:01:07,777 --> 00:01:10,450
Because of the paint that keeps sea

32
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:11,214
life away.

33
00:01:11,394 --> 00:01:13,843
It is called antifouling paint.

34
00:01:14,023 --> 00:01:15,319
For a long time,

35
00:01:15,319 --> 00:01:18,881
the most common ingredient was copper oxide,

36
00:01:18,881 --> 00:01:21,958
a copper compound that is poisonous to

37
00:01:21,958 --> 00:01:24,386
barnacles and other creatures,

38
00:01:24,386 --> 00:01:26,167
and naturally reddish.

39
00:01:26,518 --> 00:01:29,614
The paint slowly releases tiny amounts of

40
00:01:29,614 --> 00:01:32,258
copper into the water right next to

41
00:01:32,258 --> 00:01:32,938
the hull.

42
00:01:33,118 --> 00:01:36,110
Young barnacles and other larvae that try

43
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:38,664
to settle on the surface are killed

44
00:01:38,664 --> 00:01:39,759
or driven away.

45
00:01:39,939 --> 00:01:42,687
Many modern paints work in a self

46
00:01:42,687 --> 00:01:46,019
polishing way, slowly wearing off as the

47
00:01:46,019 --> 00:01:49,517
ship moves, always exposing a fresh layer.

48
00:01:49,866 --> 00:01:53,164
Today, antifouling paint can be made in

49
00:01:53,164 --> 00:01:56,545
many colours, and some ships are painted

50
00:01:56,545 --> 00:01:59,166
black, blue or grey underneath.

51
00:01:59,346 --> 00:02:01,395
But red stayed the tradition,

52
00:02:01,395 --> 00:02:03,444
partly because of the copper,

53
00:02:03,444 --> 00:02:05,917
and partly because it makes it easy

54
00:02:05,917 --> 00:02:08,390
to see where the antifouling stops.

55
00:02:08,570 --> 00:02:11,162
Red below, other colours above,

56
00:02:11,162 --> 00:02:14,004
has become the uniform of the sea.

57
00:02:14,353 --> 00:02:17,124
Why did old wooden warships have bottoms

58
00:02:17,124 --> 00:02:18,371
covered in copper?

59
00:02:18,551 --> 00:02:20,857
To protect them from a small creature

60
00:02:20,857 --> 00:02:22,103
called the shipworm.

61
00:02:22,283 --> 00:02:24,282
It is actually a kind of clam

62
00:02:24,282 --> 00:02:25,729
that bores into wood,

63
00:02:25,729 --> 00:02:28,347
eating tunnels through the planks of a

64
00:02:28,347 --> 00:02:28,692
ship.

65
00:02:28,872 --> 00:02:31,264
A wooden hull could be eaten from

66
00:02:31,264 --> 00:02:33,366
inside in just a few years in

67
00:02:33,366 --> 00:02:34,236
warm waters.

68
00:02:34,585 --> 00:02:36,455
In the seventeen sixties,

69
00:02:36,455 --> 00:02:39,297
the Royal Navy tried covering a ship's

70
00:02:39,297 --> 00:02:41,691
hull with thin sheets of copper.

71
00:02:41,871 --> 00:02:42,649
It worked.

72
00:02:42,829 --> 00:02:46,156
The copper stopped the shipworm and also

73
00:02:46,156 --> 00:02:48,568
kept weed and barnacles away,

74
00:02:48,568 --> 00:02:51,397
so ships stayed faster for longer.

75
00:02:51,577 --> 00:02:53,390
Within a couple of decades,

76
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:55,942
much of the fleet was copper bottomed.

77
00:02:56,292 --> 00:02:59,406
Copper bottomed ships could stay at sea

78
00:02:59,406 --> 00:03:02,759
longer and sail faster than their enemies,

79
00:03:02,759 --> 00:03:04,675
a real advantage in war.

80
00:03:04,855 --> 00:03:07,855
That is where the English expression copper

81
00:03:07,855 --> 00:03:09,251
bottomed comes from,

82
00:03:09,251 --> 00:03:11,763
meaning something safe and reliable.

83
00:03:11,943 --> 00:03:14,273
The red paint on modern ships is

84
00:03:14,273 --> 00:03:17,550
the direct descendant of those copper sheets.

85
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:20,180
Why was one of the most effective

86
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:22,873
ship paints ever made banned around the

87
00:03:22,873 --> 00:03:23,287
world?

88
00:03:23,467 --> 00:03:27,577
It contained a chemical called tributyltin,

89
00:03:27,577 --> 00:03:28,437
or T B T.

90
00:03:28,617 --> 00:03:30,651
From the nineteen sixties,

91
00:03:30,651 --> 00:03:33,232
it was used in antifouling paint,

92
00:03:33,232 --> 00:03:35,501
and it worked extremely well.

93
00:03:35,681 --> 00:03:38,050
Hulls stayed clean for years.

94
00:03:38,399 --> 00:03:40,201
But T B T did not only

95
00:03:40,201 --> 00:03:41,430
kill barnacles.

96
00:03:41,610 --> 00:03:44,586
It leaked into harbours and coastal waters

97
00:03:44,586 --> 00:03:46,712
and harmed a wide range of sea

98
00:03:46,712 --> 00:03:47,066
life.

99
00:03:47,246 --> 00:03:49,784
Oyster farms in France collapsed,

100
00:03:49,784 --> 00:03:51,399
with deformed shells,

101
00:03:51,399 --> 00:03:55,013
and some sea snails developed abnormal changes.

102
00:03:55,193 --> 00:03:57,575
The chemical was found to be one

103
00:03:57,575 --> 00:04:00,999
of the most toxic substances ever deliberately

104
00:04:00,999 --> 00:04:02,264
put into the sea.

105
00:04:02,613 --> 00:04:05,480
France began restricting it in the early

106
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:06,770
nineteen eighties.

107
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:10,915
The International Maritime Organization later agreed a

108
00:04:10,915 --> 00:04:13,705
global ban, which fully took effect in

109
00:04:13,705 --> 00:04:15,100
two thousand eight.

110
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:18,226
Ships had to switch to other paints,

111
00:04:18,226 --> 00:04:20,435
many based on copper again.

112
00:04:20,615 --> 00:04:23,377
A perfect solution for ships turned out

113
00:04:23,377 --> 00:04:25,572
to be a disaster for the ocean.

114
00:04:25,922 --> 00:04:28,604
Why are tiny barnacles such a big

115
00:04:28,604 --> 00:04:30,555
problem for giant ships?

116
00:04:30,735 --> 00:04:32,987
Because they make the hull rough.

117
00:04:33,167 --> 00:04:36,511
A smooth hull slides through the water.

118
00:04:36,691 --> 00:04:38,990
A hull covered in barnacles,

119
00:04:38,990 --> 00:04:41,782
weed and slime drags a thick layer

120
00:04:41,782 --> 00:04:43,178
of water with it,

121
00:04:43,178 --> 00:04:45,642
and that friction costs power.

122
00:04:45,992 --> 00:04:48,852
Heavy fouling can increase the fuel a

123
00:04:48,852 --> 00:04:51,171
ship burns by tens of percent.

124
00:04:51,351 --> 00:04:52,708
For a large ship,

125
00:04:52,708 --> 00:04:55,582
that can mean thousands of tonnes of

126
00:04:55,582 --> 00:04:57,178
extra fuel per year,

127
00:04:57,178 --> 00:04:59,094
and much more pollution.

128
00:04:59,274 --> 00:05:02,353
Navies have studied this for decades,

129
00:05:02,353 --> 00:05:05,515
because a fouled warship is slower and

130
00:05:05,515 --> 00:05:06,763
has less range.

131
00:05:07,112 --> 00:05:10,886
Barnacles are also remarkably good at sticking.

132
00:05:11,066 --> 00:05:13,797
Their glue is one of the strongest

133
00:05:13,797 --> 00:05:15,726
natural adhesives known,

134
00:05:15,726 --> 00:05:18,859
and scientists study it for medical and

135
00:05:18,859 --> 00:05:20,145
industrial uses.

136
00:05:20,325 --> 00:05:22,418
Once a barnacle has settled,

137
00:05:22,418 --> 00:05:24,586
scraping it off is hard work.

138
00:05:24,766 --> 00:05:27,389
Stopping it from landing in the first

139
00:05:27,389 --> 00:05:29,375
place is the whole strategy.

140
00:05:29,724 --> 00:05:31,806
Why do many ships have a separate

141
00:05:31,806 --> 00:05:33,951
stripe of colour at the waterline?

142
00:05:34,131 --> 00:05:36,185
It is called the boot top.

143
00:05:36,365 --> 00:05:39,031
That band of the hull is sometimes

144
00:05:39,031 --> 00:05:41,853
underwater and sometimes in the air,

145
00:05:41,853 --> 00:05:43,577
as the ship is loaded,

146
00:05:43,577 --> 00:05:46,164
unloaded, and rolls in the waves.

147
00:05:46,344 --> 00:05:47,475
It gets wet,

148
00:05:47,475 --> 00:05:51,336
dry, sunlight and scraping from docks and

149
00:05:51,336 --> 00:05:52,184
tugboats.

150
00:05:52,535 --> 00:05:55,618
Those conditions are tougher than either the

151
00:05:55,618 --> 00:05:58,281
deep underwater hull or the top sides.

152
00:05:58,461 --> 00:06:00,901
So the boot top is painted with

153
00:06:00,901 --> 00:06:02,789
a special tough coating,

154
00:06:02,789 --> 00:06:04,678
designed to resist wear,

155
00:06:04,678 --> 00:06:07,668
sunlight and fouling at the same time.

156
00:06:07,848 --> 00:06:10,117
The stripe makes it easy to see

157
00:06:10,117 --> 00:06:12,167
where that special paint is.

158
00:06:12,517 --> 00:06:13,744
On a loaded ship,

159
00:06:13,744 --> 00:06:16,777
the boot top may be completely underwater.

160
00:06:16,957 --> 00:06:18,198
On an empty ship,

161
00:06:18,198 --> 00:06:20,681
the red bottom can rise high above

162
00:06:20,681 --> 00:06:21,265
the sea.

163
00:06:21,445 --> 00:06:24,070
Watching how much red shows is a

164
00:06:24,070 --> 00:06:26,777
quick way to tell whether a cargo

165
00:06:26,777 --> 00:06:28,581
ship is full or empty,

166
00:06:28,581 --> 00:06:30,222
even from the shore.

167
00:06:30,572 --> 00:06:32,868
Why are there numbers painted down the

168
00:06:32,868 --> 00:06:34,380
front and back of a ship?

169
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:36,068
They are draft marks,

170
00:06:36,068 --> 00:06:38,294
and they show how deep the ship

171
00:06:38,294 --> 00:06:39,587
sits in the water.

172
00:06:39,767 --> 00:06:41,117
On metric marks,

173
00:06:41,117 --> 00:06:44,155
each number is ten centimetres tall,

174
00:06:44,155 --> 00:06:47,699
with gaps of ten centimetres between them,

175
00:06:47,699 --> 00:06:50,231
so the water level can be read

176
00:06:50,231 --> 00:06:51,075
precisely.

177
00:06:51,425 --> 00:06:54,557
The draft decides whether a ship can

178
00:06:54,557 --> 00:06:55,688
enter a port,

179
00:06:55,688 --> 00:06:58,386
pass through a canal or cross a

180
00:06:58,386 --> 00:06:59,517
shallow area.

181
00:06:59,697 --> 00:07:02,009
It also tells how much cargo the

182
00:07:02,009 --> 00:07:03,238
ship is carrying.

183
00:07:03,418 --> 00:07:05,647
A surveyor reads the marks at the

184
00:07:05,647 --> 00:07:07,674
bow, the middle and the stern,

185
00:07:07,674 --> 00:07:08,958
and from the depth,

186
00:07:08,958 --> 00:07:11,661
calculates the weight of cargo on board.

187
00:07:12,011 --> 00:07:14,468
This is called a draft survey,

188
00:07:14,468 --> 00:07:16,843
and for bulk cargo like coal,

189
00:07:16,843 --> 00:07:18,318
grain or iron ore,

190
00:07:18,318 --> 00:07:20,612
it is often how the cargo is

191
00:07:20,612 --> 00:07:22,332
weighed and paid for.

192
00:07:22,512 --> 00:07:25,436
A few painted numbers can decide a

193
00:07:25,436 --> 00:07:27,156
sale worth millions.

194
00:07:27,336 --> 00:07:30,149
It is one of the oldest measuring

195
00:07:30,149 --> 00:07:31,598
methods in trade,

196
00:07:31,598 --> 00:07:33,559
and still in daily use.

197
00:07:33,909 --> 00:07:36,678
Why do some modern ships have hulls

198
00:07:36,678 --> 00:07:38,339
coated in a slippery,

199
00:07:38,339 --> 00:07:39,446
rubbery layer?

200
00:07:39,626 --> 00:07:42,142
It is a foul release coating,

201
00:07:42,142 --> 00:07:44,224
often based on silicone.

202
00:07:44,404 --> 00:07:46,786
Instead of poisoning sea life,

203
00:07:46,786 --> 00:07:49,486
it makes the surface so smooth and

204
00:07:49,486 --> 00:07:52,821
slippery that creatures cannot stick well.

205
00:07:53,001 --> 00:07:54,265
When the ship moves,

206
00:07:54,265 --> 00:07:56,415
the water washes most of them off.

207
00:07:56,765 --> 00:07:59,796
These coatings release no poison into the

208
00:07:59,796 --> 00:08:02,828
sea, which makes them attractive as rules

209
00:08:02,828 --> 00:08:04,973
on toxic paints get stricter.

210
00:08:05,153 --> 00:08:07,781
They work best on ships that move

211
00:08:07,781 --> 00:08:08,976
fast and often,

212
00:08:08,976 --> 00:08:12,162
because the flowing water is what cleans

213
00:08:12,162 --> 00:08:12,560
them.

214
00:08:12,740 --> 00:08:15,165
A ship that sits still for weeks

215
00:08:15,165 --> 00:08:17,059
may still collect growth.

216
00:08:17,409 --> 00:08:20,436
A smooth hull also reduces friction,

217
00:08:20,436 --> 00:08:23,295
saving fuel even when it is clean.

218
00:08:23,475 --> 00:08:26,540
Some coatings are inspired by nature,

219
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:29,357
by the skin of sharks or dolphins,

220
00:08:29,357 --> 00:08:32,008
which stay clean without poison.

221
00:08:32,188 --> 00:08:35,046
The war against barnacles is slowly moving

222
00:08:35,046 --> 00:08:37,699
from killing them to simply giving them

223
00:08:37,699 --> 00:08:38,992
nowhere to hold on.

224
00:08:39,342 --> 00:08:41,471
Why do giant ships have to leave

225
00:08:41,471 --> 00:08:42,801
the water regularly?

226
00:08:42,981 --> 00:08:45,628
To inspect and repaint the hull.

227
00:08:45,808 --> 00:08:48,076
A dry dock is a basin that

228
00:08:48,076 --> 00:08:49,384
can be emptied.

229
00:08:49,564 --> 00:08:50,989
The ship floats in,

230
00:08:50,989 --> 00:08:52,190
the gates close,

231
00:08:52,190 --> 00:08:53,990
the water is pumped out,

232
00:08:53,990 --> 00:08:56,465
and the ship settles onto blocks,

233
00:08:56,465 --> 00:08:59,166
revealing the whole underwater hull.

234
00:08:59,516 --> 00:09:04,849
Classification societies, the organisations that certify ships

235
00:09:04,849 --> 00:09:08,548
as safe, require regular inspections of the

236
00:09:08,548 --> 00:09:08,978
hull.

237
00:09:09,158 --> 00:09:12,091
Large ships typically visit a dry dock

238
00:09:12,091 --> 00:09:13,326
every few years.

239
00:09:13,506 --> 00:09:16,617
Workers blast off old paint and growth,

240
00:09:16,617 --> 00:09:17,894
check the steel,

241
00:09:17,894 --> 00:09:21,165
the propeller, the rudder and the anodes,

242
00:09:21,165 --> 00:09:24,117
and apply fresh coats of antifouling.

243
00:09:24,468 --> 00:09:25,909
Between dry docks,

244
00:09:25,909 --> 00:09:29,272
divers and underwater robots can clean the

245
00:09:29,272 --> 00:09:30,714
hull in the water.

246
00:09:30,894 --> 00:09:32,884
But for a full inspection,

247
00:09:32,884 --> 00:09:34,644
the ship must come out.

248
00:09:34,824 --> 00:09:37,181
A stop in dry dock costs time

249
00:09:37,181 --> 00:09:40,106
and money, which is why long lasting

250
00:09:40,106 --> 00:09:43,113
hull paint is so valuable to shipping

251
00:09:43,113 --> 00:09:43,926
companies.

252
00:09:44,276 --> 00:09:46,593
Why do ships now have systems to

253
00:09:46,593 --> 00:09:48,838
treat the sea water they carry?

254
00:09:49,018 --> 00:09:51,985
Empty cargo ships take on sea water,

255
00:09:51,985 --> 00:09:55,117
called ballast, to sit deeper and stay

256
00:09:55,117 --> 00:09:55,694
stable.

257
00:09:55,874 --> 00:09:57,969
They carry it across the ocean,

258
00:09:57,969 --> 00:09:59,996
and pump it out when they load

259
00:09:59,996 --> 00:10:02,226
cargo, in a different part of the

260
00:10:02,226 --> 00:10:02,631
world.

261
00:10:02,981 --> 00:10:06,013
That water is full of tiny living

262
00:10:06,013 --> 00:10:09,505
things, larvae, plankton and bacteria.

263
00:10:09,685 --> 00:10:11,357
Released in a new place,

264
00:10:11,357 --> 00:10:13,447
some of them can spread out of

265
00:10:13,447 --> 00:10:14,004
control.

266
00:10:14,184 --> 00:10:15,685
The zebra mussel,

267
00:10:15,685 --> 00:10:18,950
carried in ballast water to the Great

268
00:10:18,950 --> 00:10:20,980
Lakes of North America,

269
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:25,304
multiplied enormously and clogged water pipes and

270
00:10:25,304 --> 00:10:28,128
power plants, costing huge sums.

271
00:10:28,478 --> 00:10:30,731
Since two thousand seventeen,

272
00:10:30,731 --> 00:10:34,772
an international convention requires ships to manage

273
00:10:34,772 --> 00:10:36,326
their ballast water,

274
00:10:36,326 --> 00:10:38,968
and many now treat it with filters

275
00:10:38,968 --> 00:10:42,931
and ultraviolet light or chemicals before releasing

276
00:10:42,931 --> 00:10:43,164
it.

277
00:10:43,344 --> 00:10:45,914
The hull keeps life out with paint,

278
00:10:45,914 --> 00:10:48,043
and the tanks keep it in with

279
00:10:48,043 --> 00:10:48,778
treatment.

280
00:10:48,958 --> 00:10:50,722
Both protect the ocean.

281
00:10:51,073 --> 00:10:54,365
Why don't shipping containers fall off ships

282
00:10:54,365 --> 00:10:55,113
in storms?

283
00:10:55,293 --> 00:10:58,100
Because each corner is locked to the

284
00:10:58,100 --> 00:10:58,880
one below.

285
00:10:59,060 --> 00:11:03,025
Every container has strong steel corner castings,

286
00:11:03,025 --> 00:11:04,320
with oval holes.

287
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:07,485
A small device called a twistlock fits

288
00:11:07,485 --> 00:11:08,821
into those holes,

289
00:11:08,821 --> 00:11:11,885
and a quarter turn locks two containers

290
00:11:11,885 --> 00:11:12,592
together.

291
00:11:12,942 --> 00:11:16,129
On deck, containers are also tied down

292
00:11:16,129 --> 00:11:19,568
with long steel bars called lashing rods,

293
00:11:19,568 --> 00:11:21,916
crossing between the stacks.

294
00:11:22,096 --> 00:11:25,790
Together, the twistlocks and lashings hold stacks

295
00:11:25,790 --> 00:11:28,277
that can be many containers high,

296
00:11:28,277 --> 00:11:31,217
against the rolling and pitching of the

297
00:11:31,217 --> 00:11:32,650
ship in heavy seas.

298
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,552
Even so, in very bad weather,

299
00:11:35,552 --> 00:11:38,457
the forces can exceed the limits,

300
00:11:38,457 --> 00:11:42,066
and containers are sometimes lost at sea,

301
00:11:42,066 --> 00:11:44,354
occasionally many at once.

302
00:11:44,534 --> 00:11:47,482
That is why ships slow down or

303
00:11:47,482 --> 00:11:49,840
change course in storms.

304
00:11:50,020 --> 00:11:53,088
Every container on the ocean is held

305
00:11:53,088 --> 00:11:55,389
by a few small steel locks,

306
00:11:55,389 --> 00:11:57,520
turned by hand in a port.

307
00:11:57,870 --> 00:11:59,309
So here are the ten.

308
00:11:59,489 --> 00:12:02,334
Red paint that keeps sea life away.

309
00:12:02,514 --> 00:12:04,731
Copper bottoms against the shipworm.

310
00:12:04,911 --> 00:12:07,314
A banned paint that harmed the ocean.

311
00:12:07,494 --> 00:12:09,329
Barnacles that burn fuel.

312
00:12:09,509 --> 00:12:11,842
A stripe for the toughest zone.

313
00:12:12,022 --> 00:12:14,031
Numbers that weigh the cargo.

314
00:12:14,211 --> 00:12:16,928
Slippery coatings without poison.

315
00:12:17,108 --> 00:12:19,882
Dry docks to inspect and repaint.

316
00:12:20,062 --> 00:12:23,336
Ballast water treated against invaders.

317
00:12:23,516 --> 00:12:26,872
And twistlocks that keep containers on board.

318
00:12:27,222 --> 00:12:29,165
The red on a ship is the

319
00:12:29,165 --> 00:12:32,160
colour of a centuries old war against

320
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:33,375
tiny creatures.

321
00:12:33,555 --> 00:12:36,687
If you want more machines explained part

322
00:12:36,687 --> 00:12:39,662
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

323
00:12:39,842 --> 00:12:41,604
And tell us in the comments,

324
00:12:41,604 --> 00:12:43,492
did you know why ships are red

325
00:12:43,492 --> 00:12:44,184
underneath?
